---
/**
 * Integrations — "Plugged in, read only".
 *
 * DESIGN: IN-C "Chip grid", Figma `wJ9DK6ByFUN6rWe0CpCVPU`, frame `152:2` on
 * page `Section — Integrations` (`150:2`). OWNER DECISION, 2026-08-02, and it
 * SUPERSEDES the earlier build of this file.
 *
 * WHAT THIS REPLACES. The previous version implemented IN-A, "The boundary
 * line": six inbound arrows, one lit rule, a READ ONLY capsule sitting on it,
 * and a struck return arrow hanging off its right-hand end. The recommendation
 * frame `157:2` had said "Ship A. Fall back to C." The owner has reviewed the
 * variants and taken C. Build it; do not re-argue it, and do not reintroduce
 * IN-A's parts one at a time — a chip grid with a struck arrow bolted onto it
 * is neither variant.
 *
 * WHAT IN-C IS, in its own words on `152:2`: "the legacy 3x3, rebuilt to the
 * craft standard: gradient border, 5% wash, specular top edge, two-colour glow.
 * Closest to what already ships and the lowest-risk to build. It states
 * read-only in words rather than drawing it, which is its one weakness."
 *
 * ══ THE ONE WEAKNESS, AND HOW IT IS COVERED ═══════════════════════════════
 *
 * That last sentence is the whole editorial problem with this variant and it is
 * worth being explicit about, because it is the thing that can quietly rot.
 * IN-A carried the read-only claim as GEOMETRY: arrows in, one struck arrow
 * out. A reader could not miss it and could not misread it. A chip grid carries
 * no direction at all, so the claim has to be carried in language, and language
 * is easier to soften later than a drawing is. Three things carry it here and
 * all three are load-bearing:
 *
 *   1. THE EYEBROW. "Plugged in, read only", above the heading.
 *   2. THE SCOPE ON EVERY CHIP. Each source prints the actual scope it is
 *      connected with — `Sites.Read.All`, `Files.Read.All`, "Sign-in only" —
 *      not the words "read only". That is STRONGER than the Figma frame, which
 *      prints "read only" nine times as a caption. A scope is checkable; a
 *      caption is an assertion. The scope line is the reason this section can
 *      claim read-only at all, and it may not be replaced with a friendlier
 *      phrase.
 *   3. THE CLAIM AND THE ASIDE beneath the grid, in two sentences that must not
 *      be merged. See below.
 *
 * ══ THE READ-ONLY CLAIM, AND WHAT WAS CORRECTED ═══════════════════════════
 *
 * CARRIED OVER UNCHANGED FROM THE IN-A BUILD, because it is accuracy rather
 * than layout and the variant switch does not touch it.
 *
 * The legacy line reads: "Read-only connectors. We never write back to your
 * files or change a setting." It sits under a 3x3 grid naming Microsoft 365,
 * Entra ID SSO, Google Workspace, Okta, Slack, Teams, Google Drive, Zapier and
 * Make — which is exactly the nine chips frame `152:2` draws, each labelled
 * "read only". Checked against the platform repo, that is FALSE for three of
 * the nine and UNSUPPORTED for two more:
 *
 *   Slack          crowagent-platform/web/lib/connectors/providers.ts:103
 *                  scopes ["chat:write", "chat:write.public", "channels:read"]
 *                  slack.ts posts via chat.postMessage. It writes.
 *   Teams          providers.ts:138, scope "ChannelMessage.Send".
 *                  ms-teams.ts sends into a channel. It writes.
 *   Google Drive   providers.ts, scope "drive.file", commented in that file as
 *                  "access ONLY to files this app creates". Narrow, but it is
 *                  still a write: the connector's whole job is export.
 *   Zapier, Make   No connector exists. CONNECTOR_PROVIDER_CONFIG holds exactly
 *                  three providers and neither is one of them. The only mention
 *                  anywhere is WorkflowBuilder.tsx:517, describing them as a
 *                  BRIDGE used to reach Slack and Teams. Naming them as
 *                  connected on the homepage claims an integration that is not
 *                  built.
 *
 * OWNER DECISION: NARROW THE LIST. So this section names only what the repo
 * evidences as read-only. The three write connectors are named in the aside
 * with their real function, because saying nothing about Slack and Teams after
 * the live site named them would read as a quiet deletion rather than a
 * correction. The two that do not exist are simply gone.
 *
 * THE GRID IS SIX CHIPS, NOT NINE, AND THAT IS THE CORRECTION MADE VISIBLE.
 * `152:2` draws nine. Building nine would put "read only" under Slack, Teams,
 * Google Drive, Zapier and Make on the homepage, which is the defect this
 * section exists to fix. Six chips in a 3x2 grid is the same composition at the
 * only length the evidence supports.
 *
 * NO JAVASCRIPT FOR CORRECTNESS. Every name, every scope, the claim and the
 * aside are in the HTML and painted at first render. `data-lit` only decides
 * when a decorative light is allowed to run along the chips' top edges, and
 * `data-band` (below) only decides whether that grid is also allowed to move.
 *
 * ══ THE TRUST BAND, A-104 ITEM 9, OWNER 2026-08-05 ════════════════════════
 *
 * "Plugged in, read only becomes a continuously autoplaying TRUST BAND instead
 * of six cards."
 *
 * ── WHAT THE BAND SHOWS, AND THE ONE THING IT REFUSES TO SHOW ───────────────
 *
 * THE SAME SIX CONNECTORS, EACH WITH THE SAME REAL SCOPE. Nothing was added and
 * nothing was taken away: `.in__track` is still HOME_CHIPS, the claim and the
 * aside beneath it are unchanged to the word, and at rest the band lays the six
 * out in the identical 3x2 grid. What changed is that when motion is allowed the
 * row travels.
 *
 * NO COMPLIANCE CLAIM WAS INVENTED, AND THAT IS THE ENTIRE CONTENT DECISION.
 * The obvious way to fill a trust band is a row of certification badges — Cyber
 * Essentials, ISO 27001, SOC 2, a GDPR mark — and this company holds none of
 * them. The owner's instruction is that an unbacked badge is WORSE than the six
 * cards it replaces, and A-114 was closed "must not add any false so ignore
 * this" for the same reason on the same day. A-88 records the harder version of
 * the same rule: SoftwareApplication was left permanently ineligible for a rich
 * result rather than have a rating invented to qualify for one. So the band's
 * trust is carried by the only material this section can evidence, which is the
 * material it already carried — a vendor's name beside the actual OAuth scope
 * we hold on it. A scope is checkable. A badge is an assertion.
 *
 * THE UK REGISTERS ARE NOT IN THE BAND EITHER, AND THAT WAS CONSIDERED. Find a
 * Tender and Contracts Finder are honestly evidenced across this site and would
 * have lengthened the band. Two things ruled them out. They exist nowhere as a
 * structured export, so putting them here means a second local list in the one
 * file whose header records what happened last time this component kept its own
 * copy of a list; and A-104 item 8 is an open owner instruction to make the
 * homepage LESS public-sector-only, per OA-25 market neutrality, which a row of
 * three UK registers pushes against. If the owner wants them, they belong in
 * src/data alongside the connectors, not typed out again here.
 *
 * ── HOW IT MOVES, AND WHY IT IS SEAMLESS BY ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN BY EYE ───
 *
 * `.in__rail` holds the track, and the script appends ONE aria-hidden clone of
 * it. Two identical copies, so a rail translated by exactly -50% has moved by
 * exactly one copy and the frame at 100% is pixel-identical to the frame at 0%.
 * There is no measurement to go stale and no resize handler to forget.
 *
 * The clone is built by the script rather than rendered, so the document a
 * reader without JavaScript receives contains each connector ONCE. That matters
 * for more than payload: a duplicated list in the markup is a list a search
 * engine and a screen reader both have to be told to ignore.
 *
 * THE SEAM CANNOT OPEN, and the sum is in `.in__chip` in band mode below.
 *
 * ── THE ACCESSIBILITY CONTRACT IS COPIED, NOT REINVENTED ────────────────────
 *
 * scripts/tabs.ts wrote this pattern for the product showcase earlier the same
 * day and this is the second caller of the same rules, deliberately phrased the
 * same way so the two controls on this page behave identically:
 *
 *   1. IT NEVER STARTS UNDER prefers-reduced-motion, checked live rather than
 *      at build time and RE-CHECKED on change, because a reader can turn the
 *      preference on with the page already open. Turning it on withdraws the
 *      band to the static grid; turning it off does not silently start moving a
 *      page somebody is already reading.
 *   2. WCAG 2.2.2 GETS ITS PAUSE CONTROL, rendered `hidden` and revealed only
 *      once the band is actually running.
 *   3. NOTHING IN THE BAND IS FOCUSABLE. A moving row that carries links or
 *      buttons puts a control under a keyboard user's caret and then takes it
 *      away again. Every chip is text, the two focusable things in this section
 *      sit still beneath it, and `overflow: hidden` on a container with no
 *      focusable descendant can never scroll a caret out of view.
 *
 * There is no equivalent of tabs.ts's third rule, the one-way stop once a reader
 * picks a tab. A band offers nothing to pick, so hover, focus and a background
 * document SUSPEND it and the pause control is the only thing that latches.
 */
import Section from '../layout/Section.astro';
import { HOME_CHIPS } from '../../data/integrations';

/**
 * THE SIX CHIPS COME FROM src/data/integrations.ts AND ARE NOT DECLARED HERE.
 *
 * They used to be. This component carried its own six-entry array, and the data
 * file carried a note saying "the next person to touch that component should
 * import SOURCES from here and delete its local array; until then, a correction
 * made in one place has to be made in both." That note also claimed the two
 * copies agreed "name for name and scope for scope". They did not, and had not
 * for some time:
 *
 *   here                  data/integrations.ts
 *   "Confluence"          "Confluence Cloud"
 *   "OneDrive"            "OneDrive for Business"
 *   "Entra ID SSO"        "Microsoft Entra ID"
 *   "Sign-in only"        "SAML 2.0, sign-in only"
 *
 * Confluence Cloud and Confluence Data Center are different products, and
 * personal OneDrive is not supported at all — so the homepage was naming two
 * connectors more loosely than the page it links to, and the section's own rule
 * that a scope is quoted rather than described had quietly become a friendlier
 * phrase. Nothing could see it, because there was nothing to compare.
 *
 * HOME_CHIPS is a selection of the SAME OBJECTS the /integrations page renders,
 * not a matching list. Every name, scope and mark on this section is now
 * corrected in one place or not at all. The evidence for each connector — the
 * file and line in the platform repo that proves its scope — lives with the data
 * rather than being restated here.
 *
 * SIX, NOT NINE: see the note in data/integrations.ts and the correction record
 * above. Do not lengthen this grid from the Figma frame.
 */
---

{/* `sv-floor`, on the <section> so it runs the full viewport width. The
    reasoning trace above is base and the closing card below sits on the lit
    step, so the ladder still alternates. See styles/surfaces.css. */}
<Section
  eyebrow="Plugged in, read only"
  title="Your evidence already exists. We read it where it lives."
  id="integrations"
  class="sv-floor"
>
  {/*
    `data-lit` at threshold 0.35. The grid is complete before it means anything:
    every chip, every name and every scope is drawn at full strength. This
    attribute only decides when a light is allowed to run along the top edges
    that are already there.
  */}
  {/* The connector glyph's gradient, defined once for the six chips. Same
      decision as /integrations, and the reasoning is written out there: one mark
      for every connector because seven vendor marks were withdrawn under A-37
      and A-38 and cannot come back, and a GRADIENT across the ambient ramp
      rather than a flat hue because flat teal means evidenced and flat violet
      means refused. A status on this site is always one colour, so a two-colour
      sweep cannot be mistaken for one. */}
  <svg width="0" height="0" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" class="in__defs">
    <defs>
      <linearGradient id="in-grad" x1="0" y1="1" x2="1" y2="0">
        <stop offset="0" stop-color="var(--c-ambient-start)" />
        <stop offset="1" stop-color="var(--c-ambient-end)" />
      </linearGradient>
    </defs>
  </svg>

  <div class="in" data-spot="off" data-lit data-lit-threshold="0.35">
    <div class="in__amb" data-field aria-hidden="true">
      <b class="in__amb-a"></b>
      {/* The cursor light and the grain, inside the wrapper so both stay behind
          every word and inherit its mask. <i>, so neither joins the drift. */}
      <i class="sv-spot"></i>
      <i class="sv-grain"></i>
    </div>

    {/*
      A LIST OF NAMES WITH THEIR SCOPES, NOT A LOGO WALL. Each source is a real
      text node with its real scope beside it, so the section reads correctly
      with every pixel of the chip treatment removed. That was true of the IN-A
      build too and it matters more here, because the scope line is now the
      section's per-source evidence rather than a caption beside a drawing.

      THE BAND IS A WRAPPER AROUND THE SAME LIST, NOT A REPLACEMENT FOR IT. At
      rest `.in__track` is the identical 3x2 grid this section has always drawn.
      Only when the script has confirmed motion is allowed does the wrapper
      become a moving row. See the trust-band note in the header.

      `--in-count` is HOME_CHIPS.length rather than a typed 6, because the
      seamlessness of the loop is an arithmetic consequence of it. See
      `.in__chip` in band mode below, where the sum is written out.
    */}
    <div class="in__band" data-band="off" style={`--in-count:${HOME_CHIPS.length}`}>
      {/*
        `surface--ramp` IS THE BAND'S GROUND, AND IT IS THE SITE'S OWN PANEL
        RECIPE RATHER THAN A NEW ONE. Owner, 2026-08-05: *"in home page section
        Plugged in, read only has moving trust band so can we make it dark
        gradient as text are white so background gradient must be dark"*.

        Until now the six chips sat straight on `.sv-floor`, so the band had no
        ground of its own: a row of tiles floating on the section colour. What it
        wants is the thing styles/surfaces.css already defines for "a panel that
        HOLDS cards" — a vertical ramp from --c-panel down to very nearly the
        floor, so the band sits ON something at its head and sinks back into the
        page at its foot. That is one class, no new recipe, and every stop in it
        is a token.

        IT IS DARK AT BOTH ENDS, WHICH IS THE OWNER'S ACTUAL REQUIREMENT, and the
        figures are in the `.in__viewport` note in the stylesheet below rather
        than asserted here.

        NEITHER BRAND HUE APPEARS IN IT. Teal means verified and violet means
        refused; a panel behind six connector names asserts neither, and the
        chips already carry the ramp's two ends on their own 1px edge, which is
        where the colour on this section belongs.
      */}
      <div class="in__viewport surface--ramp">
        <div class="in__rail" data-band-rail>
          <ul class="in__track" data-band-track>
            {
              HOME_CHIPS.map((s) => (
                <li class="in__chip">
                  {/*
                    THE SPECULAR, AND IT IS ON THE SAFEST SURFACE IN THE SECTION.
                    A 1px highlight travelling the chip's TOP EDGE only. Nothing
                    sits on that edge and nothing reads across it, so light there
                    costs no contrast anywhere. Sweeping the chip's FILL would put
                    light under the name and the scope, which the motion rules
                    forbid outright. Clipped by the chip's own overflow, so it can
                    never widen the page while it travels — the failure that is
                    invisible to any check measuring a settled layout.
                  */}
                  <span class="in__spec" aria-hidden="true" />

                  <span class="in__logo in__logo--mark" aria-hidden="true">
                    <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" focusable="false">
                      <g fill="none" stroke="url(#in-grad)" stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                        <path d="M10 13.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 0 5 0l3-3a3.54 3.54 0 0 0-5-5l-1.5 1.5" />
                        <path d="M14 10.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 0-5 0l-3 3a3.54 3.54 0 0 0 5 5l1.5-1.5" />
                      </g>
                    </svg>
                  </span>
                  <span class="in__name">{s.name}</span>
                  <span class="in__scope">{s.scope}</span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/*
        THE PAUSE CONTROL, AND IT IS RENDERED `hidden`. WCAG 2.2.2 requires a way
        to pause anything that moves automatically for longer than five seconds,
        and this band never stops on its own. The control is revealed by the
        script BELOW, only once the band is genuinely running, so a reader with
        JavaScript off or prefers-reduced-motion set is never handed a button
        that pauses nothing. Exactly the contract TabSwitcher.astro states for
        `.tabsw-auto__pause`, which is the same pattern on the same page.

        THE LABEL IS "Pause scrolling" AND NOT "Pause". The homepage already
        carries the product showcase's pause control, whose accessible name is
        "Pause". Two controls on one document answering to the same name is a
        defect a screen-reader user meets and nobody else does.
      */}
      <div class="in__transport">
        <button class="in__pause" type="button" data-band-pause hidden>
          <span class="in__pause-icon" aria-hidden="true" />
          <span data-band-pause-label>Pause scrolling</span>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>

    {/*
      THE CLAIM, NARROWED TO WHAT THE REPO EVIDENCES. Two sentences, and the
      split between them is the whole correction. The first is a guarantee about
      the six sources drawn above. The second names the three connectors that DO
      write, with their real function, so nothing on this page implies a
      read-only guarantee over a surface that posts messages or creates files.

      DO NOT MERGE THESE INTO ONE SENTENCE and do not restore the legacy blanket
      wording. With IN-A's struck arrow gone this pair is doing more work than it
      was: it is now the only place the read-only boundary is stated as a
      boundary rather than as a scope value. See the header note.
    */}
    <p class="in__claim">
      Every source above is read only. We do not write to your files, change a
      setting in your tenant, or create an account.
    </p>
    <p class="in__aside">
      Slack, Teams and Google Drive are separate outbound connectors, used to send
      a notification or export a file you have asked for. They are not sources we
      read from.
    </p>

    <p class="in__more">
      <a href="/security">How we handle your data</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Section>

<script>
  /*
   * THE BAND'S ONLY JOB IS TO DECIDE WHETHER THE ROW IS ALLOWED TO MOVE.
   *
   * Nothing here is load-bearing for correctness. Every connector name, every
   * scope, the claim and the aside are in the document the server sent and are
   * painted before this file is fetched. With JavaScript off, `data-band` stays
   * `off`, not one band rule in the stylesheet matches, and the reader gets the
   * 3x2 grid this section has always drawn. That is the same guarantee the
   * header note makes about `data-lit`.
   *
   * ── IT IS THE tabs.ts CONTRACT, SECOND CALLER ───────────────────────────────
   *
   * scripts/tabs.ts wrote this for the product showcase on the same page and on
   * the same day, and this is deliberately the same shape rather than a second
   * opinion about how autoplay should behave. Three things it copies exactly:
   *
   *   1. THE PREFERENCE IS CHECKED LIVE, AND AGAIN ON CHANGE. Not at build time,
   *      because a reader can turn prefers-reduced-motion on with the page
   *      already open, and a loop that keeps running through that is precisely
   *      the complaint the preference exists to make. Turning it ON withdraws
   *      the band to the static grid and takes the pause control away with it.
   *      Turning it OFF does nothing: this never calls start() from the change
   *      handler, because beginning to animate a page somebody is already
   *      reading is not an improvement.
   *   2. THE PAUSE CONTROL IS REVEALED HERE, NOT RENDERED VISIBLE. WCAG 2.2.2
   *      requires it, and it is unhidden only at the moment the row genuinely
   *      starts moving, so a reader who never gets the band never gets a button
   *      that pauses nothing.
   *   3. HOVER, FOCUS AND A BACKGROUNDED DOCUMENT SUSPEND RATHER THAN STOP.
   *      Passing a pointer over the band is not a decision, so the row resumes
   *      on leave. Only the button latches.
   *
   * The one rule NOT copied is tabs.ts's third: autoplay there stops for good
   * once a reader picks a tab, because continuing to advance after someone has
   * expressed a preference takes the page off them. A band offers nothing to
   * pick, so there is no such choice to honour and no one-way stop.
   *
   * ── THE CLONE ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * Exactly one, aria-hidden, appended to the rail. Two identical copies is what
   * makes a -50% translation land pixel-identically on the frame it started
   * from; the arithmetic is written out in the stylesheet. It is built here
   * rather than rendered so the document contains each connector once: a
   * duplicated list in the markup is a list a crawler and a screen reader both
   * have to be told to ignore, and `data-band-track` is stripped from the copy
   * so nothing can ever find two.
   */
  const reducedMotion = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');

  /* EVERY ONE OF THESE QUERIES IS TYPED, and that is not decoration. The bare
     forms return `Element` and `Node`, neither of which has `dataset`, `hidden`,
     `setAttribute` on a clone, or `remove()`, so the untyped version of this
     block produced ten TypeScript errors and failed the whole build. Astro
     type-checks the code inside a <script> exactly as it does a .ts file. */
  for (const band of document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-band]')) {
    const rail = band.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-band-rail]');
    const track = band.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-band-track]');
    const pauseBtn = band.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-band-pause]');
    const pauseLabel = pauseBtn
      ? pauseBtn.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-band-pause-label]')
      : null;
    /* No rail, no track or no control is not a band. Leave the grid alone. */
    if (!rail || !track || !pauseBtn) continue;

    /** The appended copy, or null while the band is at rest. */
    let clone: HTMLElement | null = null;
    /** Latched by the button. Distinct from a hover or visibility suspend. */
    let paused = false;

    /* Holds the row exactly where it is. `animation-play-state`, so resuming
       continues from the same frame instead of jumping back to the start. */
    const suspend = () => band.setAttribute('data-band-suspended', '');
    const resume = () => {
      if (paused) return;
      band.removeAttribute('data-band-suspended');
    };

    const start = () => {
      if (reducedMotion.matches || band.dataset.band === 'on') return;
      /* `cloneNode` is declared as returning `Node`, which carries neither
         setAttribute nor remove(). The cast is safe by construction: `track` is
         already an HTMLElement above, and a deep clone of one is one. */
      clone = track.cloneNode(true) as HTMLElement;
      clone.setAttribute('aria-hidden', 'true');
      clone.removeAttribute('data-band-track');
      rail.appendChild(clone);
      band.dataset.band = 'on';
      pauseBtn.hidden = false;
      pauseBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(paused));
      if (paused) suspend();
      else resume();
    };

    /* Back to the resting grid, with the copy removed and the control withdrawn.
       The reader's own `paused` choice is kept, so if the band ever runs again
       in this document it honours it. */
    const withdraw = () => {
      if (band.dataset.band !== 'on') return;
      band.dataset.band = 'off';
      band.removeAttribute('data-band-suspended');
      if (clone) {
        clone.remove();
        clone = null;
      }
      pauseBtn.hidden = true;
    };

    pauseBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
      paused = !paused;
      if (paused) suspend();
      else resume();
      pauseBtn.setAttribute('aria-pressed', String(paused));
      pauseBtn.classList.toggle('is-paused', paused);
      if (pauseLabel) {
        pauseLabel.textContent = paused ? 'Resume scrolling' : 'Pause scrolling';
      }
    });

    for (const ev of ['pointerenter', 'focusin']) band.addEventListener(ev, suspend);
    for (const ev of ['pointerleave', 'focusout']) band.addEventListener(ev, resume);

    document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
      if (document.visibilityState === 'hidden') suspend();
      else resume();
    });

    if (typeof reducedMotion.addEventListener === 'function') {
      reducedMotion.addEventListener('change', () => {
        if (reducedMotion.matches) withdraw();
      });
    }

    start();
  }
</script>

<style>
  .in {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    /* ONE GAP, READ BY BOTH LAYOUTS. The grid and the moving row have to agree
       on it or the band's period stops being a whole number of chips. It was a
       literal on the grid rule until the band arrived; the value is unchanged. */
    --in-gap: clamp(12px, 1.4vw, 18px);
    /* How far in from each end the band fades out. Wide enough at 1440 that a
       chip is unreadable before it reaches the edge, small enough at 390 that it
       never eats a whole chip. */
    --in-fade: clamp(24px, 7vw, 120px);
  }

  /* ── Ambient, per the lighting rig (Figma `141:2` L03) ───────────────────*/
  /* Bleed exactly one gutter, never a percentage. The containing block is the
     section body, which is the measure inset by --gutter, so this reaches the
     viewport edge at every width below the measure and stops there. A
     percentage bleed overflowed the page at 390 and 768.

     `data-field` opts the field into the shared ambient layer declared in
     styles/tokens.css: the always-on --m-drift, and the one-beat m-arrive
     entrance every section on this page shares. There were two fields until
     2026-08-05; see `.in__amb-a` below. */
  .in__amb {
    position: absolute;
    /* Scroll-driven parallax throw. See styles/tokens.css. */
    --par: 24px;
    inset-block: -18%;
    inset-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    z-index: -1;
    overflow: hidden;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* All four edges are masked. `overflow: hidden` stops the bleed widening
       the page but cuts a 150px blur off square, and a blurred field ending in
       a straight line draws a visible lighter rectangle. One radial mask fades
       every edge at once, so there is no axis for it to miss. See the full note
       in MarketShape.astro, including the mistake of masking only one axis.  */
    /*
     * `closest-side`, NOT `115% 105%`, AND THAT IS A MEASURED CORRECTION.
     *
     * The earlier value was chosen to fade all four edges at once, which it
     * did — but only partly. An ellipse of 115% x 105% has semi-axes of 0.575w
     * and 0.525h, so the middle of the left edge sits at r = 0.5 / 0.575 =
     * 0.87 of the way along a ramp that only reaches transparent at 1.0. About
     * 25% of the field survives there. On a 150px blur whose own falloff is
     * nearly finished, 25% of nearly nothing is nothing, which is why it held.
     *
     * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §3 raises the orb stops by roughly 1.5x to
     * 1.9x, and 25% of that is a visible vertical line. Measured on the built
     * result at 1440: a lighter RECTANGLE across this section and the four
     * others sharing this recipe, one gutter in from each side — exactly the
     * defect the original note describes and thought it had closed.
     *
     * `closest-side` gives semi-axes of 0.5w and 0.5h, so the ramp reaches
     * transparent EXACTLY at the middle of every edge and past it at every
     * corner. There is no arithmetic left to get wrong, and it stays correct
     * when a section changes shape.
     */
    -webkit-mask-image: radial-gradient(
      closest-side,
      rgb(0 0 0) 42%,
      transparent 100%
    );
    mask-image: radial-gradient(closest-side, rgb(0 0 0) 42%, transparent 100%);
  }

  .in__amb b {
    position: absolute;
    border-radius: 50%;
    filter: blur(150px);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* ONE FIELD, IN VIOLET. It was teal high-left and violet low-right, the pair
     five homepage sections were running at once, which is what A-113 measured:
     the most saturated tokens on badges, borders and fields simultaneously,
     where a premium dark interface reserves peak saturation for one focal
     point per section. This section's focal points are six evidenced sources
     and the teal on their marks, so the light gives its hue up and they keep
     theirs. Geometry and opacity are the high-left field's, unchanged, so the
     band is lit from the same direction at the same level. */
  .in__amb-a {
    width: 560px;
    height: 400px;
    left: -6%;
    top: -26%;
    background: var(--orb-violet);
    opacity: 0.5;
  }

  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .in__amb b { filter: blur(90px); width: 320px; height: 280px; }
  }

  /* ── The chip grid, which is also the band's resting state ───────────────
     THREE COLUMNS, TWO ROWS. `152:2` draws a 3x3; six evidenced sources fill
     3x2, which is the same composition at the only length the evidence
     supports. See the header note on why it is not nine.

     THIS IS WHAT EVERY READER GETS BEFORE ANYTHING RUNS, and what a reader with
     prefers-reduced-motion or with JavaScript off keeps: all six sources, all
     six scopes, laid out and legible, nothing clipped and nothing off-screen.
     The band in the block further down is an upgrade applied on top of it, not
     a different way of rendering the same content. `.in__rail` is a plain <div>
     with no declarations at all up here, so at rest it generates a box and
     changes nothing.

     `.in__viewport` NO LONGER IS ONE, and the paragraph above said it was until
     2026-08-05. It now carries the band's ground in BOTH states — see the block
     directly below. */

  /* ══ THE BAND'S GROUND ════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Owner, 2026-08-05: the trust band should sit on a dark gradient, because its
   * text is white. The recipe is `surface--ramp` from styles/surfaces.css,
   * applied in the markup rather than restated here — the site's standard
   * vertical panel ramp, --c-panel at the head falling to
   * color-mix(--c-bg 92%, --c-floor) at the foot. Not one colour is written in
   * this file.
   *
   * WHY THAT RAMP AND NOT A NEW ONE. surfaces.css defines it for exactly this
   * shape: "a panel that HOLDS cards sinks back into the page at its foot so the
   * cards sit on top of something rather than beside it". Six chips on a band is
   * that shape. Writing a second dark ramp here would be the tenth gradient
   * recipe the token block exists to prevent.
   *
   * MEASURED at 1440, from the RENDERED PIXELS of a screenshot rather than from
   * this stylesheet, sampling the band's ground on a column clear of every chip.
   * The second figure in each row is the same pixel with this ramp switched off
   * in the live document, so the delta is this change and nothing else:
   *
   *                        with the ramp        without (the ground until now)
   *     head    reduce     #0C0F20  19.01:1     #05060F  20.20:1
   *     foot    reduce     #0E0A21  19.37:1     #110D24  18.97:1
   *     head    band on    #0C0F20  19.01:1     #04050E  20.32:1
   *     foot    band on    #0F0B21  19.26:1     #0F0D23  19.05:1
   *
   * against #FFFFFF ink. The ramp costs 1.2 points at its head, buys 0.3 back at
   * its foot, and every one of the eight readings is above 18.9:1 — an order of
   * magnitude clear of the 4.5:1 floor. That is the whole answer to "is it dark
   * enough": the ground under this band is still effectively black.
   *
   * THROUGH THE CHIP FILL, which is the pair a reader actually reads, six chips
   * sampled 10px inside the fill at mid-height:
   *
   *     .in__name  #FFFFFF        5.61:1 to 5.86:1   passes
   *     .in__scope --c-text-sub   4.03:1 to 4.21:1   FAILS 1.4.3 (needs 4.5:1)
   *
   * THAT SCOPE-LINE FAILURE IS NOT THIS RAMP AND IT IS NOT NEW, and it is
   * recorded here rather than quietly left because the same element has now had
   * a contrast figure written about it twice and been wrong twice. Measured
   * without the ramp the same six samples read 4.11:1 to 4.34:1, so this change
   * moves the worst of them by 0.20 of a point and the element was already
   * below the floor.
   *
   * ITS CAUSE IS THE CHIP'S OWN TWO-BACKGROUND TRICK, DIRECTLY BELOW. That trick
   * paints --grad-edge to the BORDER box under a fill clipped to the PADDING
   * box, and its comment says "the ramp shows only in the 1px the border
   * occupies". It does not, and it cannot: the fill is
   * color-mix(--c-ambient-start 5%, --c-card) over --c-card, which computes to
   * about 10% alpha, so --grad-edge — teal at 48%, violet at 34% — reads
   * straight through the whole face of every chip. Measured, the chip fill is
   * about #317174: a saturated teal, not a 5% wash. That is why A-84 sampled
   * "#3a5e69 to #3e5c69" and called it "the chip's own teal gradient"; it was
   * the border ramp leaking, and the 5.06:1 A-84 recorded for --c-text-sub on it
   * is 4.03:1 when the pixels are read.
   *
   * IT IS LEFT ALONE HERE ON PURPOSE. Making that fill opaque is a one-line fix
   * and it is also a visible redesign of six tiles the owner has been looking
   * at, plus a hard rule question — teal means verified and violet means
   * refused, and both are currently decorating a connector list. It belongs to
   * the owner, not to a change about the band's ground.
   *
   * THE RAMP IS ONLY LIGHTER THAN THE FLOOR AT ITS HEAD, and that is the point:
   * --c-floor is #03040A, so a band drawn on it had no edge at all. The head of
   * the ramp is nine units of blue above the floor, which is a seat rather than
   * a panel shouting.
   *
   * ── THE BLEED AND THE PADDING MOVED UP HERE FROM BAND MODE ────────────────
   *
   * One gutter of bleed, never a percentage, for the reason the ambient field
   * above argues at length. It was declared inside the `no-preference` block so
   * only a MOVING row got it; now that this element paints, a reader under
   * prefers-reduced-motion has to get the same panel or the section has two
   * different grounds depending on a preference.
   *
   * `padding-inline: var(--gutter)` gives the bleed straight back to the
   * content, so the resting 3x2 grid measures exactly what it measured before
   * this block existed and not one chip moves. Band mode takes the padding off
   * again, because a row that stopped a gutter short of the fade would read as a
   * wide card rather than as a row that continues.
   *
   * `padding-block` bought back with a negative `margin-block`, so the panel
   * stands --stack-2 clear of the chips at both ends and the section costs no
   * height for it. Same declaration band mode already carried, for the same
   * reason: a hard horizontal edge across a chip's glow is the straight-edge-on-
   * a-blur defect the ambient mask exists to avoid.
   *
   * NO `border-radius`. In band mode the ends are masked to nothing, so a corner
   * there is a value that can never be seen; at rest the panel is one gutter
   * wider than the measure at every width and reads as a band. A radius would be
   * a card's signal on a thing that is deliberately not a card — the same
   * argument `.in__chip` already makes for refusing `.surface`.
   */
  .in__viewport {
    margin-inline: calc(-1 * var(--gutter));
    padding-inline: var(--gutter);
    padding-block: var(--stack-2);
    margin-block: calc(-1 * var(--stack-2));
  }

  .in__track {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    gap: var(--in-gap);
  }

  /* ══ THE BAND ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * TRIGGER    `data-band="on"`, written by the script at the foot of this file
   *            and ONLY when `prefers-reduced-motion` does not match. It is
   *            withdrawn again if the reader turns the preference on.
   * CADENCE    three cycles of --m-cycle for one full copy, which is 36s for
   *            roughly 1,340px: about 37px a second. Slow enough that a scope
   *            can be read as it passes, which is the whole point of the row.
   * PLAYS      loops, with no rest beat. The owner asked for CONTINUOUS.
   * REDUCED    the grid above, unmoved and complete.
   *
   * EVERY RULE IN HERE IS DOUBLY GATED — this media query AND the attribute.
   * The attribute alone would be enough, because the script checks the same
   * preference before writing it, but a layout this destructive if it ran at the
   * wrong moment should not depend on one line of JavaScript being correct.
   * Under `reduce` there is no band rule in the sheet at all.
   *
   * IT IS EXPRESSED IN --m-cycle RATHER THAN IN SECONDS for the reason the
   * specular block below already gives: the section's place in the page's
   * rhythm survives its drawing being replaced.
   */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    /*
     * THE BLEED AND THE VERTICAL PADDING ARE NO LONGER DECLARED HERE. They were,
     * with the argument for both written out at length, and both moved up to the
     * base `.in__viewport` rule on 2026-08-05 when that element gained the band's
     * dark ground: a panel that only existed while the row was moving would give
     * a reader with prefers-reduced-motion a different section. The reasoning is
     * unchanged and now lives beside the rule that carries it.
     *
     * WHAT IS LEFT HERE IS THE THREE THINGS THAT ARE TRUE ONLY OF A MOVING ROW.
     *
     * `padding-inline: 0` HANDS THE GUTTER BACK. At rest the padding exists to
     * keep the resting grid at exactly the measure; a moving row wants the full
     * bled width, so the chips travel to the fade and not to an invisible inset.
     * Without this the row would stop one gutter short of each end and read as a
     * wide card, which is the failure the bleed was written against in the first
     * place.
     *
     * `overflow: hidden` CLIPS THE TRAVEL, and the negative margin-block above
     * is what stops it shearing the chips' two-colour glow off square along the
     * top and bottom — the straight-edge-on-a-blur defect the ambient mask
     * exists to avoid.
     */
    .in__band[data-band='on'] .in__viewport {
      padding-inline: 0;
      overflow: hidden;
      /* Both ends fade rather than cut. A chip sliced in half by a hard edge
         reads as a rendering fault; a chip fading out reads as a row that
         continues. rgb(0 0 0) rather than a token because a mask is a stencil
         and not a colour: only its alpha is ever sampled. Same call, and the
         same wording, as the ambient mask above.

         IT NOW TAKES THE GROUND WITH IT, AND THAT IS RIGHT RATHER THAN A COST.
         A mask applies to everything the element paints, so `surface--ramp`
         fades out at the two ends along with the chips travelling over it. The
         alternative is a panel whose ends are cut off square inside a row that
         fades, which is the same defect on the other axis. One mask, one fade,
         the whole band. */
      -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent 0,
        rgb(0 0 0) var(--in-fade),
        rgb(0 0 0) calc(100% - var(--in-fade)),
        transparent 100%
      );
      mask-image: linear-gradient(
        90deg,
        transparent 0,
        rgb(0 0 0) var(--in-fade),
        rgb(0 0 0) calc(100% - var(--in-fade)),
        transparent 100%
      );
    }

    .in__band[data-band='on'] .in__rail {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: nowrap;
      width: max-content;
      animation: in-band calc(var(--m-cycle) * 3) linear infinite;
    }

    /* The one thing the pause control does, and the only thing hover, focus and
       a backgrounded document do. Pausing the ANIMATION rather than removing it
       holds the row exactly where it stopped, so resuming does not jump. */
    .in__band[data-band='on'][data-band-suspended] .in__rail {
      animation-play-state: paused;
    }

    /*
     * THE TRAILING GAP IS PART OF THE COPY, AND IT IS WHAT MAKES -50% EXACT.
     * The rail lays its two copies out with no gap of its own; each copy carries
     * its own gap after its last chip. So one copy measures
     * (count x chip) + (count x gap) and the rail measures twice that, which
     * means a rail translated by -50% has advanced by one whole copy including
     * the space between the last chip and the first. Put the gap on the RAIL
     * instead and the period becomes copy + half a gap, and the seam drifts by
     * half a gap on every repeat.
     */
    .in__band[data-band='on'] .in__track {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: nowrap;
      gap: var(--in-gap);
      padding-inline-end: var(--in-gap);
    }

    /*
     * AND THE SEAM CANNOT OPEN, WHICH IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN A JUDGEMENT.
     * A gap would appear only if one copy were narrower than the band, and one
     * copy measures at least
     *
     *     count x (--measure / count)  +  count x --in-gap
     *   = --measure + count x --in-gap
     *
     * while the band itself is at most --measure wide, by the bleed argument
     * above. So a copy is always wider than the window by at least the whole of
     * the gap budget, at every viewport, and it stays true if --measure moves or
     * if HOME_CHIPS gains an entry, because both are read rather than assumed.
     * The 200px floor only ever raises the left-hand term.
     */
    .in__band[data-band='on'] .in__chip {
      flex: 0 0 auto;
      min-width: max(200px, calc(var(--measure) / var(--in-count)));
    }
  }

  /* Starts AT rest and travels away from it, which is a loop and not an
     entrance: check-motion.js rule 2 asks whether the first offset translates
     and the last resolves to zero, and this is the other way round. The element
     is never missing from anywhere it belongs. */
  @keyframes in-band {
    from { transform: translate3d(0, 0, 0); }
    to   { transform: translate3d(-50%, 0, 0); }
  }

  /* ── The pause control ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   *
   * WCAG 2.2.2. The band moves for as long as the page is open, so a way to stop
   * it is mandatory rather than a courtesy, and it is rendered `hidden` so it can
   * never be a dead control (see the markup note).
   *
   * IT IS UNPAINTED, AND THAT IS A DESIGN CALL WITH A GATE CONSEQUENCE WORTH
   * KNOWING. A transport control governs the component it sits in; it is not an
   * action this page offers, and this section already has one real destination
   * in `.in__more`. Giving it a fill would put a second button-shaped thing
   * under a row of six tiles that deliberately refuse to look clickable. It is
   * therefore styled as `.in__more a` is — 44px of target, body ink, no box —
   * which is also why scripts/check-render.js does not read it as a bespoke
   * button: that rule only reaches controls with horizontal padding AND a
   * painted background or border, and this has neither.
   *
   * NEITHER BRAND HUE APPEARS ON IT. Teal means verified and violet means
   * refused; a pause button asserts neither. Same rule, same wording, as
   * `.tabsw-auto__icon`.
   */
  .in__transport {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .in__pause {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size, the same token `.in__more a` takes. */
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    padding: 0;
    border: 0;
    background: none;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    font-weight: 700;
    line-height: var(--lh-tight);
    cursor: pointer;
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  .in__pause:hover { color: var(--c-text); }

  .in__pause:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* Drawn from borders rather than shipped as an icon: two bars for pause, one
     triangle for play. Copied from `.tabsw-auto__icon` on purpose — two
     transport controls on one page that draw themselves differently is exactly
     the drift the design system exists to stop. */
  .in__pause-icon {
    width: 10px;
    height: 12px;
    border-inline: 3px solid currentColor;
    box-sizing: border-box;
  }

  .in__pause.is-paused .in__pause-icon {
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    border: 6px solid transparent;
    border-inline-start: 10px solid currentColor;
    border-inline-end: 0;
  }

  /*
   * A CHIP, NOT A SMALL CARD, AND THE DISTINCTION STILL DECIDES WHETHER IT
   * TAKES `surface`. It does not, for the same reason as before: a card is a
   * destination and this is a label with a value beside it — no heading, no
   * action, nothing to click. `surface` would bring --radius-card, --blk-pad, a
   * backdrop filter and a hover lift, and would make six labels look like six
   * things you can open. What IN-C asks for is four properties, and every one
   * of them comes from a token:
   *
   *   gradient border    the double-background trick below
   *   5% wash            a --c-teal color-mix over --c-card
   *   specular top edge  --gloss-inset, the same one-token highlight the cards
   *                      and now the buttons take
   *   two-colour glow    teal core, violet halo, the ramp's two ends
   *
   * THE GRADIENT BORDER IS TWO BACKGROUNDS, NOT A PSEUDO-ELEMENT. The fill is
   * painted to the padding box and the ramp to the border box, with the border
   * itself transparent, so the ramp shows only in the 1px the border occupies.
   * A ::before overlay would need its own stacking and would sit over the
   * travelling specular; this needs neither and costs one declaration.
   */
  .in__chip {
    position: relative;
    display: grid;
    /* ── CENTRED, AND THE THIRD TIME OF ASKING ────────────────────────────
     *
     * This was a two-column grid — an 18px logo column with the name and scope
     * left-aligned beside it — and the owner has now reported it twice. The
     * first fix corrected the placeholder's SIZE (an 8px dot in an 18px slot)
     * and reported the icons as centred. They were: each icon sat in the middle
     * of its own column. The question asked was about the CELL, and the answer
     * given was about the icon.
     *
     * DESIGN-DECISIONS.md: card content centres, because it is scanned rather
     * than read. A chip is scanned. One column, everything centred. (The
     * `surface--read` opt-out this used to cite was retired on 2026-08-03 when
     * the owner reversed the decision behind it; see styles/alignment.css.)
     *
     * WHY THE GATE DID NOT CATCH IT, WHICH IS THE PART THAT MATTERS. Rule 1
     * fires on `text-align: left | start` being DECLARED. This chip never
     * declared it. It achieved left alignment structurally, through the column
     * template, and inherited `start` from the document. A gate that reads
     * declarations cannot see geometry. scripts/check-render.js now measures
     * the rendered box instead, and this chip is the defect it was written for
     * — its ALLOW entry names "a chip whose 18px logo column pushed its label
     * off the axis" as the case the rule exists to catch.
     *
     * IT SAID "Rule 8 in check-design-system.js" UNTIL 2026-08-04, and that is
     * two errors in five words. check-design-system.js launches no browser at
     * all — it says so in its own header and imports only node:fs, node:path
     * and node:url — so it cannot measure a rendered box; and its rule 8 is the
     * light-scope token check on `.art__pane`, which has nothing to do with
     * alignment. A citation naming a gate that cannot do the thing is worse
     * than no citation: it closes the question. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: auto auto auto;
    row-gap: 6px;
    justify-items: center;
    text-align: center;
    padding: 16px;
    border: 1px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--radius-chip);
    /* Two tokens, because the chip paints two gradients at once and they are
       two different recipes: --grad-ramp is the surface wash on the padding
       box, --grad-edge is the coloured 1px border on the border box. They
       cannot be one token — two var() references on one element resolve the
       same knobs. */
    --ramp-angle: 160deg;
    --ramp-from: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-ambient-start) 5%, var(--c-card));
    --ramp-to: var(--c-card);
    --ramp-end: 78%;
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
    /* ── THE FILL IS OPAQUE NOW, AND THAT IS A WCAG FIX RATHER THAN A RESTYLE ─
     *
     * This used to be color-mix(--c-ambient-start 5%, --c-card) over --c-card.
     * --c-card is rgba(255,255,255,0.05), so the whole padding-box fill computed
     * to about 10% alpha and DID NOT MASK the border-box ramp underneath it. The
     * rule's own comment claimed "the ramp shows only in the 1px the border
     * occupies". It never did: --grad-edge, teal at 48% and violet at 34%, read
     * straight through the face of every chip. Measured from decoded pixels the
     * face was #317174, a saturated teal, and --c-text-sub on it is 4.03:1
     * against the 4.5:1 WCAG 1.4.3 requires. A-84 recorded 5.06:1 for this
     * element; that figure was taken from the token rather than the pixels and
     * is wrong.
     *
     * TWO RULES WERE BEING BROKEN AT ONCE, which is why this is not a taste
     * call. It failed AA on six tiles, and teal means VERIFIED on this site
     * while violet means REFUSED, so a connector list was wearing both for
     * decoration. Making the fill opaque fixes both by making the code do what
     * it already said it did: the ramp returns to the 1px border, the face stops
     * being teal, and the scope line gets its contrast back.
     *
     * LUMINANCE ONLY, NO HUE. --c-raised to --c-panel is two opaque steps of the
     * same neutral, which is the treatment the audit of 2026-08-05 recommends
     * for any gradient sitting UNDER TEXT. The 5% ambient tint is gone with it;
     * a chip carrying two lines of text is exactly where a hue does not belong. */
    background:
      linear-gradient(160deg, var(--c-raised), var(--c-panel) 78%) padding-box,
      var(--grad-edge) border-box;
    /* The specular top-light plus a two-colour glow: a tight teal core under a
       wide violet halo. One colour reads as a blur; two read as light, and both
       are points on the ramp this page already runs. */
    box-shadow:
      var(--gloss-inset),
      0 0 18px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-ambient-start) 12%, transparent),
      0 14px 40px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-ambient-deep) 22%, transparent);
    /* Clips the travelling specular to the chip's own top edge. */
    overflow: hidden;
    transition:
      background-image var(--dur) var(--ease),
      box-shadow var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  /* ══ THE CHIP ANSWERS A CURSOR ════════════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * Six chips, ZERO :hover rules — one of the four sections
   * specs/ULTRA-PREMIUM-GAP.md §2.6 names, and the most obviously dead of them,
   * because a grid of six tiles is the shape a reader most expects to respond.
   *
   * WHAT A CHIP DOES: its wash steps up from --c-card to the fourth surface
   * value, its gradient border opens towards both ends of the ramp, its
   * two-colour glow roughly doubles, and its scope line comes up from
   * --c-text-muted to --c-text-sub.
   *
   * THE VENDOR MARK DOES NOT RESPOND, AND MAY NOT BE MADE TO. It used to: the
   * marks sat at `opacity: .86` at rest and came to 1 on hover, which reads well
   * and is not ours to do. These are other companies' registered trade marks and
   * several of the vendors named here forbid exactly this in writing — Slack's
   * brand terms rule out changing transparency, Microsoft's forbid altering the
   * symbol, Atlassian's require the logo "depicted exactly as shown … without
   * any modification (aside from re-sizing)". A rest state that dims every mark
   * on the page is a modification applied sitewide. Removed 2026-08-04. If the
   * row ever looks too loud, change what is AROUND the marks.
   *
   * IT DOES NOT LIFT, AND THAT IS THE ONE THING TO PRESERVE IF THIS IS EVER
   * EDITED. A chip is a label with a value beside it: no heading, no action,
   * nothing to click. styles/surfaces.css restricts the card lift to elements
   * that actually navigate precisely so a page cannot advertise six affordances
   * that are not there, and this section's header note already refuses
   * `surface` for the same reason. Light, never movement.
   *
   * BOTH TEXT ENDPOINTS CLEAR THE FLOOR: --c-text on --c-lit is about 19:1 and
   * --c-text-sub about 12:1, so every intermediate frame of the transition
   * clears it too, which is what the axe gate samples for.
   */
  .in__name,
  .in__scope {
    transition: color var(--dur) var(--ease);
  }

  @media (hover: hover) {
    /* The lit state is the same two recipes at a higher light level, not a
       second pair: only the knobs move. background-image rather than the
       shorthand, so the padding-box / border-box origins set above survive. */
    .in__chip:hover {
      --ramp-from: color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-ambient-start) 7%, var(--c-lit));
      --ramp-to: var(--c-raised);
      --edge-a: 78%;
      --edge-b: 62%;
      /* Written here, not var(--grad-ramp). A custom property's var()s are
         substituted where it is DECLARED, so a knob token on :root bakes in its
         fallbacks and inherits finished. Measured: --grad-ramp computes to
         `linear-gradient(180deg, #161B2F, #0E1220 100%)` on every element,
         whatever knobs it sets. It hid every blog hero photo behind an opaque
         rectangle before this was found. Colours still come from tokens. */
      /* ── NEUTRAL, BECAUSE THIS IS A HOVER ────────────────────────────────
       *
       * This lit state used to resolve from --c-ambient-start and
       * --c-ambient-deep, which are teal and violet: a 7% teal tint in the
       * fill, a 26px teal glow at 22%, and a 38% VIOLET cast shadow. Teal means
       * VERIFIED on this site and violet means REFUSED, and the standing rule
       * is that neither may appear on a hover, a focus ring or a control fill.
       * Six connector chips were lighting in both semantic hues because a mouse
       * moved over them.
       *
       * It is the same defect the hover audit of 2026-08-05 found on
       * `.sv-raised`, where nine drawn panels opened a violet glow, and it was
       * missed there for the same reason: the tokens are named for the AMBIENT
       * FIELD rather than for their hue, so `--c-ambient-deep` does not read as
       * "the refusal colour" at the call site. It resolves to #7C3AED.
       *
       * All three now resolve from --c-interactive, which V6 puts at white, so
       * the lit state is a LUMINANCE change - which is exactly what the
       * gradient rule approved on 2026-08-05 permits anywhere, including under
       * text. The cast shadow takes --c-floor instead: a shadow is an absence
       * of light, so a white one was never right regardless of the hue rule.
       *
       * The glow drops 22% to 12% with the hue change. White at the old alpha
       * reads far brighter than a saturated teal at the same number, which is
       * the same correction the audit measured on `.sv-raised` when it took
       * violet 26% to white 14%. */
      background-image:
        linear-gradient(160deg, color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-interactive) 7%, var(--c-lit)), var(--c-raised) 78%),
        var(--grad-edge);
      box-shadow:
        inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.16),
        0 0 26px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-interactive) 12%, transparent),
        0 18px 48px color-mix(in srgb, var(--c-floor) 38%, transparent);
    }

    /* A-84: the rest state moved up to --c-text-sub to clear 4.5:1 on the
       chip's own gradient, so the lit state moves up with it. A hover that
       resolved to the same value as rest would be a lift that does nothing. */
    .in__chip:hover .in__scope { color: var(--c-text); }
    .in__more a:hover { color: var(--c-text); }
  }

  .in__logo {
    /* Row 1 of three now, not a column spanning two rows. */
    width: 18px;
    height: 18px;
    object-fit: contain;
  }

  /*
   * THE PLACEHOLDER KEEPS THE FULL 18px SLOT, and that is the fix rather than a
   * detail. It used to shrink the box to 8px and re-centre it with a 5px inline
   * margin. Geometrically that is centred, and it was: measured, the dot sat on
   * exactly the same axis as every logo. It still read as misaligned, because a
   * row of six icons is judged on optical mass, not on box centres, and one
   * icon carrying less than a fifth of the area of its neighbours breaks the
   * row however well it is positioned.
   *
   * So the slot is identical for every chip and the dot is painted inside it.
   * A gradient rather than a child element because there is no child to style:
   * the placeholder is an empty aria-hidden span, and adding one would put a
   * second node in the markup for a decorative mark.
   *
   * It reads at the same strength as the marks beside it, which it did not until
   * 2026-08-04: the vector marks were held at 86% and this dot at 100%, so the
   * one slot with nothing in it was the brightest thing in the row. The marks are
   * now served unmodified, at full strength, for the reason set out above the
   * hover block, and the dot simply matches them.
   */
  /* Replaced `.in__logo--dot`, a 4px teal disc that stood in wherever a vendor
     mark was not available. Every chip now carries the same glyph, so there is
     no longer a placeholder state to style — which is the point: a grid where
     one chip has artwork and five have a dot reads as five gaps.
     drop-shadow rather than box-shadow, kept from the dot: it follows the
     PAINTED shape, where box-shadow would ring the 18px box and halo the empty
     corners of a glyph that does not fill it. */
  /* FILLS THE SLOT, DOES NOT RESIZE IT. First written as `width: 18px;
     height: 18px`, which check-design-system.js failed — correctly. `.in__logo`
     already declares the 18px slot, so restating the number on the modifier is
     a second place for it to be wrong, and the gate's rule is exactly that: a
     modifier paints INSIDE the slot, it does not set the box. 100% inherits
     whatever the slot is, so changing the slot changes the glyph with it. */
  .in__logo--mark svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    filter: drop-shadow(0 0 6px var(--c-ambient-glow));
  }

  .in__defs {
    position: absolute;
    width: 0;
    height: 0;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .in__name {
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--c-text);
  }

  /* The scope. Mono, because it is a machine value quoted verbatim rather than
     a description of one, and it is this section's per-source evidence now that
     the struck arrow has gone with IN-A.

     A-84, WCAG 1.4.3. This was --c-text-muted, and the note here said it
     "measures 9.88:1 on --c-bg". THAT FIGURE WAS TRUE AND IT WAS MEASURED
     AGAINST THE WRONG SURFACE. These chips do not sit on --c-bg: each one
     paints its own teal gradient, sampled from the rendered pixels at #3a5e69
     to #3e5c69, and --c-text-muted on THAT measures 3.31:1 to 3.50:1 against
     the 4.5:1 this 12-13px text needs. Six elements, both viewports, twelve
     failing measurements, and the best sample anywhere inside the text boxes
     was 4.32:1 — no part of the gradient passed.
     --c-text-sub computes about 5.06:1 on the same ground. The hover lift moves
     up to --c-text rather than disappearing, so the chip still brightens.
     THE LESSON IS THE COMMENT, NOT THE COLOUR: a contrast figure is a claim
     about a PAIR, and quoting it against the page background when the component
     paints its own is how a real failure ships with a passing number beside it. */
  .in__scope {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* ── The claim ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────*/
  .in__claim {
    margin: var(--sec-gap) auto 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.8;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--c-text-sub);
  }

  .in__aside {
    margin: var(--stack-1) auto 0;
    max-width: 66ch;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    line-height: 1.8;
    text-align: center;
    color: var(--c-text-muted);
  }

  .in__more {
    margin: var(--stack-2) 0 0;
    text-align: center;
  }

  .in__more a {
    /* 44px, WCAG 2.2 target size. */
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    min-height: var(--btn-h-sm);
    font-family: var(--font-body);
    font-size: var(--t-body);
    color: var(--c-interactive);
  }

  .in__more a:focus-visible {
    outline: 3px solid var(--c-interactive);
    outline-offset: 3px;
    border-radius: 2px;
  }

  /* ══ THE LIGHT ALONG THE CHIP EDGES ═══════════════════════════════════════
   *
   * TRIGGER IntersectionObserver, threshold 0.35.
   * CADENCE one beat of travel per chip, a sixth of a beat apart. The sixth
   * chip lands at two beats.
   * PLAYS: ONCE. It looped, on --m-cycle, until 2026-08-05; see the rule below
   * for why twelve perpetual sweeps were the homepage's animation ratchet.
   * REDUCED MOTION nothing travels and nothing is missing.
   *
   * THE FIGURE CHANGED WITH THE VARIANT; THE CADENCE DID NOT. IN-A ran a light
   * down six inbound arrows at one beat each, a sixth of a beat apart. IN-C has
   * no arrows, so the same light runs along six chip TOP EDGES on exactly the
   * same clock. That is the point of expressing cadence in stations rather than
   * in milliseconds: the section's drawing was replaced wholesale and its place
   * in the page's rhythm did not move. See the motion grammar in
   * styles/tokens.css.
   *
   * WHITE CORE, TEAL BLOOM. A lamp, not a marker (`141:2` L04), and the same
   * band the hero rail, the spine, the both-sides beams and the closing card's
   * sweep all use.
   *
   * A NOTE ON WHAT IS NO LONGER HERE. The IN-A build carried a standing rule
   * that the struck return arrow must never animate, because a refusal is a
   * fact and not a performance. IN-C has no struck arrow to hold still, so that
   * rule now lives entirely on the reasoning trace and the both-sides rail,
   * where the refusal markers still fire once and never loop. Nothing in this
   * section is a refusal any more; the read-only boundary is carried in words
   * and in six printed scopes, and neither of those moves at all.
   */
  .in__spec {
    /* No animation and no trigger: zero pixels. */
    display: none;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .in__spec {
      display: block;
      position: absolute;
      /* The chip's own top edge, inside its radius. */
      top: 0;
      inset-inline: 0;
      height: 1px;
      z-index: 1;
      /* --grad-beam at its default width and hue: the same travelling light as
         the reasoning rail and the both-sides spine, which is the point. */
      background: var(--grad-beam);
      opacity: 0;
    }

    /* ONE PASS EACH, ON REVEAL, AND THIS RULE WAS THE WHOLE RATCHET.
     *
     * A-111 measured the homepage's perpetual animation count climbing as the
     * page is read and never coming back down, and this line was most of it:
     * twelve `.in__spec` elements each starting an INFINITE animation the
     * moment the section was first revealed, and no mechanism anywhere to stop
     * one. Scrolling back to the top did not help, because `data-lit` is set
     * once and deliberately never unset — motion.ts says so at length, and it
     * is right to: unsetting it would restage every one-shot beat on this page
     * each time a reader scrolled past a section twice.
     *
     * So the fix is not a switch, it is the shape of the animation. This is
     * exactly what BothSides.astro did to its seven figures under A-104, in the
     * same words: an animation that spends nineteen twentieths of its life
     * holding a rest state is one document.getAnimations() goes on counting.
     * Measured on the built page at 1440: twelve running loops after the read,
     * zero after this change, and the sweep a reader sees is identical.
     *
     * THE ARITHMETIC IS UNCHANGED TO THE MILLISECOND. The window was 0% to 5%
     * of --m-cycle, and --m-cycle is twenty beats, so the pass was already
     * exactly one beat long. The duration is that beat now and the keyframe
     * percentages below are the old ones multiplied by twenty. */
    [data-lit='on'] .in__spec {
      animation: in-spec var(--m-beat) var(--m-ease-travel) 1;
    }

    /* A sixth of a beat apart, in reading order, so the six chips fill exactly
       one beat between the first starting and the last starting.

       THIS NOTE USED TO ARGUE THE STAGGER FROM THE REPEAT — "a delay on an
       infinite animation is a permanent phase offset, so the stagger survives
       every repeat rather than only the first". There is no repeat now, so
       what the delays buy is the one thing they always bought: the order the
       six chips light in. The five values are untouched. */
    [data-lit='on'] .in__chip:nth-child(2) .in__spec { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) / 6); }
    [data-lit='on'] .in__chip:nth-child(3) .in__spec { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 2 / 6); }
    [data-lit='on'] .in__chip:nth-child(4) .in__spec { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 3 / 6); }
    [data-lit='on'] .in__chip:nth-child(5) .in__spec { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 4 / 6); }
    [data-lit='on'] .in__chip:nth-child(6) .in__spec { animation-delay: calc(var(--m-beat) * 5 / 6); }
  }

  /* ONE BEAT OF TRAVEL, AND THE BLOCK IS NOW THAT BEAT AND NOTHING ELSE.
     The window was 0% to 5% of a twenty-beat cycle with the last nineteen
     twentieths of the block holding the light parked off the far edge; the
     duration is one beat now, so 1% of the cycle is 20% of the pass and 4% is
     80%. Every fade lands at the same instant it did, on the same easing,
     because the four offsets kept their ratios exactly.

     THE LAST OFFSET IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE RESTING STATE. The light starts
     translated off one edge at opacity 0 and ends translated off the other at
     opacity 0: it crosses and leaves, and never resolves to where the element
     lives. That is what keeps this a figure rather than an entrance, and it is
     what check-motion.js rule 2 tests for. */
  @keyframes in-spec {
    0%    { transform: translateX(-50%); opacity: 0; }
    20%   { opacity: 1; }
    80%   { opacity: 1; }
    100%  { transform: translateX(50%); opacity: 0; }
  }

  /* Windows high-contrast throws the gradient border and the glow away and
     paints its own colours. The chip has to keep an edge, because the edge is
     the only thing separating one source from the next. */
  @media (forced-colors: active) {
    .in__chip {
      border-color: CanvasText;
      box-shadow: none;
    }

    /* The fade is decoration. High contrast mode exists to remove exactly this
       kind of softening, and a reader who has asked for it should not have the
       ends of a moving row dimmed on them. The band still moves and is still
       clipped by its own box; only the gradient goes. */
    .in__band[data-band='on'] .in__viewport {
      -webkit-mask-image: none;
      mask-image: none;
    }
  }

  /* ── Narrower ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Three columns need roughly 210px each before `Google Workspace` and
     `Sites.Read.All` start breaking badly. Two, then one. Nothing is hidden at
     any width: unlike IN-A's arrow row, which depended on six sources sitting
     in one line and had to be dropped below 1024, a chip grid simply reflows,
     and the light along the top edges reflows with it.

     THESE GOVERN THE RESTING GRID ONLY, and they have to: they are plain
     `.in__track` rules at one class of specificity, where every band rule is
     written `.in__band[data-band='on'] .in__track` and sits inside a
     `no-preference` query. A band does not reflow — that is what a band is —
     and at 390 it simply shows fewer chips at a time. */
  @media (max-width: 767px) {
    .in__track { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr)); }
  }

  @media (max-width: 420px) {
    .in__track { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  }
</style>
